# Covers: a `finally` body that sleeps PARKS the strand and resumes later,
#         carrying the unwind it suspended through to the end.
#
# This fixture used to pin the opposite: the old core treated a yield
# inside cleanup as an unrecoverable "cleanup overflow" fatal, because a
# finally body ran there as a nested VM invocation and parking one had
# nowhere to go back to.  In the re-founded runtime a finally is an
# ordinary continuation of the same frame (core design section 9): the
# walker leaves a marker holding the suspended unwind, the body runs as
# normal code, and OP_RESUME hands the unwind back.  Parking in the middle
# of that is no different from parking anywhere else, so the fatal is gone
# and the cleanup is no longer truncated.
#
# The tag-scope block is forked off with `, 0` so the sleep lands on a
# detached strand and the `## host: step` verdicts are about that strand
# rather than about the driver's own run.
#
# Sequence: t.stop() raises a STOP; the walker finds the try's finally and
# runs it; sleep(1s) parks, so the step reports a pending deadline.  Once
# the clock passes it the body finishes (b9 becomes 2), OP_RESUME restores
# the STOP, the tag scope matches it, and the strand runs off the end --
# nothing left, so the VM is quiescent.
## timeout: 30
## host: run var t = Tag.new()
## host: run Realm.b9 = 0
## host: run t: { try { t.stop() } finally { sleep(1s); Realm.b9 = 2 } }, 0
## host: step 1000
## host: run Realm.b9
## host: advance-clock 1500
## host: step 1000
## host: run Realm.b9
[00000001] <Tag>
[00000002] 0
[00000003] 0
[00000004] step: WAKE_AT
[00000005] 0
[00000006] step: QUIESCENT
[00000007] 2
